//BUG="printing a bound void value still emits a void local and a void parameter"
//OUT="()"

// `core/io.cpl` declares `fn print x: void`, which prints `()`, so a void value
// is printable like any other. What this case is waiting on is the same codegen
// erasure as the rest of the void cases here: both the void local `r` and the
// void parameter of `print` still reach the emitted C, and gcc rejects them.
//
// This case used to live at 05_functions/73e_printing_a_bound_void_value.cpl
// and assert the opposite — that `out "{r}"` had no candidate and was rejected.
// It was written as the guard against erasing void from the type system as well
// as from codegen. The void `print` overload settles that question the other
// way: void is a printable value, so the case is now an //OUT case.
//
// 66e_using_the_value_of_a_void_function.cpl keeps the remaining half of that
// guard — a void value is printable, but still not usable in arithmetic.
//
// When this goes green it belongs in 12_stdlib with the other `print`
// overloads, not back in 05_functions.

fn nothing
    ret

fn main
    r = nothing()
    out "{r}"
